1. Model Weights
Weights are the learned parameters of an AI model.

2. Activations
Activations are intermediate values created while the model processes input tokens.

More input tokens can require more activation memory and Larger batch sizes generally increase activation memory.
Efficient memory management helps avoid GPU out-of-memory errors.

3. KV Cache
KV cache stores previously calculated Key and Value tensors during text generation.
As it Avoids recomputing previous tokens, Makes autoregressive generation more efficient and Uses additional GPU memory as the sequence grows.

4. Memory Fragmentation
Memory fragmentation happens when GPU memory is divided into small unused or poorly arranged blocks.
Even if some memory is technically free, allocating a large continuous block can become difficult.

KV cache memory:
2 \times L \times B \times S \times H_{KV} \times D \times Bytes

Where:
* L = Number of layers
* B = Batch size
* S = Sequence length
* Hkv = Number of KV heads
* D = Head dimension
* Bytes = Bytes per element (2 for FP16)

The factor 2 represents Key and Value.
